> explain K Means CLustering

● K-Means Clustering

  ---
  What is it?

  An unsupervised machine learning algorithm that groups data into K clusters based on similarity — with no labeled data
  needed.

  ---
  Real-Life Analogy

  A mall wants to group 10,000 customers into segments based on spending habits. K-Means automatically groups them into
  clusters like Budget Shoppers, Regular Shoppers, Premium Shoppers.

  ---
  How it Works — Step by Step

  Step 1: Choose K (number of clusters)
          K = 3

  Step 2: Randomly place K centroids (cluster centers)
          ★ ★ ★  (3 random points)

  Step 3: Assign each data point to nearest centroid
          Each point → closest ★

  Step 4: Recalculate centroid = mean of all points in cluster
          ★ moves to center of its group

  Step 5: Repeat Steps 3 & 4 until centroids stop moving
          → Convergence reached

  ---
  Visual Example

  Before Clustering:          After K-Means (K=3):

    o o o x x x               🔴🔴🔴 🔵🔵🔵
    o o x x x z               🔴🔴 🔵🔵🔵 🟢
    o x x z z z               🔴 🔵🔵 🟢🟢🟢
    z z z z z z               🟢🟢🟢🟢🟢🟢

  ---
  Python Implementation

  import pandas as pd
  import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
  from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
  from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

  # Load data
  df = pd.read_csv(r'C:\Users\SriniAchuthan\OneDrive\Desktop\Python\vgsales.csv\vgsales.csv')
  df = df.dropna()

  # Features
  X = df[['NA_Sales', 'EU_Sales', 'JP_Sales', 'Global_Sales']]

  # Scale features
  scaler = StandardScaler()
  X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)

  # Apply K-Means
  kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=3, random_state=42)
  df['Cluster'] = kmeans.fit_predict(X_scaled)

  # Visualize
  plt.scatter(df['NA_Sales'], df['EU_Sales'], c=df['Cluster'], cmap='viridis')
  plt.xlabel('NA Sales')
  plt.ylabel('EU Sales')
  plt.title('K-Means Clustering - Video Game Sales')
  plt.colorbar(label='Cluster')
  plt.show()

  ---
  Finding Optimal K — Elbow Method

  inertia = []

  for k in range(1, 11):
      km = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42)
      km.fit(X_scaled)
      inertia.append(km.inertia_)

  plt.plot(range(1, 11), inertia, marker='o')
  plt.xlabel('Number of Clusters (K)')
  plt.ylabel('Inertia')
  plt.title('Elbow Method — Finding Optimal K')
  plt.show()

  Pick K where the curve bends like an elbow — that's the optimal K.

  ---
  Key Terms

  ┌─────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │    Term     │                    Meaning                     │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ K           │ Number of clusters to form                     │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Centroid    │ Center point of a cluster                      │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Inertia     │ Sum of distances from points to their centroid │
  ├─────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Convergence │ When centroids stop moving                     │
  └─────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Advantages vs Disadvantages

  ┌──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │          Advantages          │               Disadvantages               │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Simple and fast              │ Must specify K manually                   │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Scales to large data         │ Sensitive to outliers                     │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Easy to interpret            │ Assumes spherical clusters                │
  ├──────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Works well with numeric data │ Different runs may give different results │
  └──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  When to Use

  Customer Segmentation    → Group customers by behavior
  Document Clustering      → Group similar articles
  Image Compression        → Reduce image colors
  Anomaly Detection        → Find unusual data points
  Market Segmentation      → Group products by sales pattern

